Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so it reads tab-separated input. Make one pull request that meets every acceptance line below.

Current state (read these first):
- bin/csv2json.js reads stdin and calls parse(readFileSync(0, 'utf8')) with no separator. It ignores all arguments.
- src/parse.js exports parse(text, sep = ','). Splitting on the separator is naive, and you can reuse this parameter.
- test/csv.test.js uses node:test and has a 'cli' test that runs execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }). npm test runs `node --test`.
- README.md shows only `node bin/csv2json.js < table.csv`.

Steps:
1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv.slice(2) includes '--tsv'. The flag may appear anywhere among the arguments. If it is there, call parse(text, '\t'). If not, call parse(text) exactly as today. Keep the output format unchanged: JSON.stringify(...) followed by a single '\n'. Do not change how unknown arguments are handled, add --help, add a general --sep/--delimiter option, auto-detect the separator, or add quoting rules.
2. Leave src/parse.js alone unless you really need to change it. If you do change it, its behaviour without the flag must stay the same.
3. In test/ (for example test/csv.test.js), add at least one node:test test that runs bin/csv2json.js with the '--tsv' argument on tab-separated input through execFileSync and asserts the exact output. Suggested cases: 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]\n'; 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' -> '[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]\n'; 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]\n'. Also add a test that tab input without the flag still gives '[{"a\\tb":"1\\t2"}]\n' (the JSON tab escape). Keep the existing tests.
4. In README.md, document the --tsv flag with an example invocation such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`, and say that it reads tab-separated input. Keep the CSV example.
5. Run npm test and each of the commands below, and confirm the results.

Acceptance lines (all must hold):
- `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.
- With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]` followed by a newline.
- With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]` followed by a newline.
- Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline.
- Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a\tb":"1\t2"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.
- `npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.
- README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.